From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PS3 Linux project <ps3@localhost>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 23:03:44 +0300
Subject: Fewer GL calls and draws a frame: shared mesh buffers, items in a
 row, batched background billboards

For the PS3's PPE, where Mesa's per-call cost dominates. Each change has a
switch, on unless its environment variable is 0; NEVERBALL_GL=0 turns them
all off and gives the original call stream:

- NEVERBALL_GL_MESH: all meshes of a solid file in one vertex and one element
  buffer (16-bit elements rebased; files over 65536 vertices keep a buffer per
  mesh), the five item files in one shared buffer, so buffers and pointers are
  set once for meshes drawn in a row instead of once a mesh.
- NEVERBALL_GL_ITEMS: items drawn in a row with the same matrices and fewer
  pushes, no depth mask changes around missing billboards, and the mesh state
  kept from one item to the next.
- NEVERBALL_GL_BACK: the background billboards transformed on the CPU into one
  client array, one draw per run of the same material in the same order
  (plain materials only; lit, environment-mapped, point-sprite and shadowed
  ones keep the old path).
- NEVERBALL_GL_STATE: no depth state changes around empty transparent passes;
  only the server or only the client texture unit selected where only one is
  used.

gui/demo1.nbr on llvmpipe at GL 2.1: 2154 -> 844 calls and 137 -> 74 draws a
frame; the same pixels except rounding at background billboard edges.

